      "back_to_stem_lab_menu": "Back to STEAM Lab menu",
      "bat_anatomy_click_a_part_to_learn_more": "🦠 Bat Anatomy — Click a part to learn more",
      "bat_biology": "Bat Biology",
      "bat_biology_the_auditory_specialist": "Bat Biology — the auditory specialist",
      "bat_diet_diversity_not_just_bugs_and_f": "🍽️ Bat Diet Diversity — Not Just Bugs and Fruit!",
      "bat_echolocation": "Bat Echolocation",
      "bat_echolocation_at_source_up_to_140_d": "Bat echolocation at source (up to 140 dB!)",
      "bat_hunting_buzz_phase": "Bat Hunting: Buzz Phase",
      "bat_hunting_strategy_the_three_phases": "Bat Hunting Strategy — The Three Phases",
      "bat_myths_busted": "Bat Myths — Busted!",
      "bat_range": "← Bat range →",
      "bat_saliva_anticoagulants_inspired_str": "Bat saliva anticoagulants inspired stroke medications. Bat immune systems fight viruses without getting sick, teaching us about disease resistance. Bat sonar inspires technology for blind navigation devices.",
      "bat_species_gallery": "🦇 Bat Species Gallery",
      "bat_species_worldwide": "bat species worldwide",
      "bat_wing": "Bat Wing",
      "bats_are_killed_by_wind_turbines_not_j": "Bats are killed by wind turbines not just from blade strikes, but from barotrauma — the rapid pressure drop near spinning blades causes their lungs to expand and blood vessels to burst. Migratory species like the hoary bat are most at risk. Solutions include curtailing turbines during peak migration and low-wind nights when bats are most active.",
      "bats_emit_ultrasonic_clicks_20_120_khz": "Bats emit ultrasonic clicks (20–120 kHz, above human hearing) and read echo delay + frequency shift to build a 3D scene at 100+ targets per second. Donald Griffin coined “echolocation” in 1944.",
      "bats_emit_ultrasonic_pulses_20_200_khz": "Bats emit ultrasonic pulses (20-200 kHz) from their mouths or noses. These sound waves bounce off objects and return to the bat’s ears. The time delay tells the bat how far away objects are, and the frequency shift (Doppler effect) tells it whether objects are moving toward or away. In this 3D simulation, each sonar pulse you emit travels outward and illuminates cave walls and moths as it passes them — just like a real bat’s sonar cone!",
      "bats_use_cf_for_cluttered_environments": "Bats use CF for cluttered environments, FM for open. Investigate why.",
      "beetle": "Beetle",
      "better_at_soaring_and_gliding": "• Better at soaring and gliding",
      "beyond_echolocation_bat_superpowers": "👀 Beyond Echolocation — Bat Superpowers",
      "bioacoustics": "Bioacoustics",
      "bioacoustics_explorer": "🔊 Bioacoustics Explorer",
      "biology_sections": "Biology sections",
      "bird_wing": "Bird Wing",
      "blue_whale_call_loudest_animal": "Blue whale call (loudest animal)",
      "both_bats_and_birds_fly_but_their_wing": "Both bats and birds fly, but their wings evolved completely independently. The differences reveal how evolution solves the same problem in different ways:",
      "both_bats_and_dolphins_evolved_echoloc": "Both bats and dolphins evolved echolocation independently — a textbook case of convergent evolution! Despite being separated by 80 million years of evolution, they use remarkably similar strategies: emit high-frequency pulses, listen for echoes, and build a \"sound picture\" of their environment. Even the neural circuits that process echoes are similar, suggesting evolution has found an optimal solution to the sonar problem.",
      "both_strategies_are_real_insectivorous": "Both strategies are real! Insectivorous bats evolved high-powered laryngeal echolocation for active hunting. Most fruit bats rely on vision and smell, but Egyptian fruit bats are the exception — they developed tongue-click echolocation independently.",
      "bright_sharp_echoes": "Bright, sharp echoes",
      "brighter_echoes_closer_objects_or_hard": "Brighter echoes = closer objects or harder materials. Faint flickering echoes = soft-bodied insects (moths). The glow fades over 1-2 seconds, simulating how the bat's \"mental image\" decays between pulses.",
      "build_a_bat_box": "Build a bat box",
      "buildings_give_rock_hard_echoes_street": "Buildings give rock-hard echoes. Streetlights create confusing bright spots. Moths cluster around lights.",
      "c_f_at_60_khz_in_air_5_7_mm_fine_enoug": "λ = c / f. At 60 kHz in air, λ ≈ 5.7 mm — fine enough to resolve a moth’s wing. Lower frequencies travel further but resolve coarser; higher = sharper but absorbed quickly. Trade-off baked in.",
      "can_change_wing_shape_dramatically_hig": "• Can change wing shape dramatically (high maneuverability)",
      "can_fold_flat_against_body_birds_can_p": "• Can fold flat against body — birds can perch and walk",
      "can_make_tighter_turns_than_any_bird": "• Can make tighter turns than any bird",
      "cannot_fold_flat_bats_must_hang_upside": "• Cannot fold flat — bats must hang upside down to rest",
      "cars_use_ultrasonic_sensors_40_khz_to_": "Cars use ultrasonic sensors (40 kHz) to detect obstacles — a direct application of bat echolocation principles. The \"beeping\" speeds up as you get closer, mimicking a bat's buzz phase!",
