      "machine_switched": "Showing ",
      "machine_switched_camera": ". Camera reset to the overview.",
      "machine_trebuchet": "Trebuchet",
      "machines_fired": "machines fired",
      "manual_e2": "The ledger in the Build and Test Range views shows this chain for the machine you have configured. The table view carries exactly the same numbers as the bars.",
      "manual_e_g35": "You do work at the crank. That work gets stored, either by lifting a heavy weight or by twisting rope. When you release it, the store spends its energy on the stone. At every step a little is lost, so the stone never gets everything you put in.",
      "manual_e_g68": "Follow the joules. Work in at the crank equals force times distance. Winch friction takes a share, so what is stored is less than what you did. At release the stored energy is shared between the stone and everything else that has to move, which is why a heavy arm is expensive. Then air resistance takes more on the way down.",
      "manual_e_g912": "The chain is a sequence of efficiencies multiplied together. Transfer efficiency is m_p / (m_p + m_eff), where m_eff is the machine inertia reduced to the payload radius. That single expression explains why a light projectile leaves fast but wastes most of the store, and why a heavy one is efficient but slow. Range is not maximised at either end, but only once drag is present.",
      "manual_e_k2": "Machines do not make energy. They move it around. You put energy in when you crank the handle, and the machine gives most of it back to the stone. Some always gets lost on the way.",
      "manual_h_intro": "A note on how we know any of this. Ancient and medieval engineers left far fewer numbers than we would like, so most of what follows comes from a small number of surviving technical treatises, from archaeology, and from modern reconstructions. Where scholars disagree, this manual says so rather than picking a side.",
      "manual_m1": "Every siege engine here is built out of the same six simple machines you met in the Machine Shop.",
      "manual_m2": "None of them adds energy. Each one only changes the exchange rate between force and distance.",
      "manual_m_lever": "Lever: the throwing arm itself. The pivot is the fulcrum, and the ratio of the two arms sets how much faster the payload end travels.",
      "manual_m_pulley": "Pulley: the block and tackle on the cocking rope, multiplying the crew’s pull.",
      "manual_m_ramp": "Inclined plane: the ramp the ammunition is rolled up, and the wedge under the frame that sets elevation.",
      "manual_m_screw": "Screw: the tensioning gear on a torsion engine, adjusting the spring by tiny amounts against a huge load.",
      "manual_m_wedge": "Wedge: the trigger and the pawl of the ratchet, which hold an enormous force with a small one.",
      "manual_m_wheel": "Wheel and axle: the windlass that cocks the machine. A big handle turning a small drum.",
      "manual_route_energy_action": "Open the 3D Test Range",
      "manual_route_energy_copy": "Make a prediction, fire the stone, and follow the joules from the crank to the flight.",
      "manual_route_energy_title": "Watch the energy move",
      "manual_route_eyebrow": "Step back into the world",
      "manual_route_history_action": "Open the 3D Target Wall",
      "manual_route_history_copy": "Aim at a target and see what a reconstruction can show—and what history still has to tell us.",
      "manual_route_history_title": "Put the model against a wall",
      "manual_route_machines_action": "Visit the Machine Shop",
      "manual_route_machines_copy": "Tune a bench, watch force and distance trade places, then prove your prediction.",
      "manual_route_machines_title": "Try one simple machine",
      "manual_route_model_action": "Experiment in the Test Range",
      "manual_route_model_copy": "Change drag, gravity, angle, or wind and watch which conclusions survive.",
      "manual_route_model_title": "Stress-test the model",
      "manual_route_record_action": "Open Compare",
      "manual_route_record_copy": "Compare the three machines side by side and record what the ledger makes you think.",
      "manual_route_record_title": "Turn notes into a claim",
      "manual_x1": "This tool is a teaching model, and it is worth being precise about where it stops.",
      "manual_x2": "What the model does get right is the shape of the trade-offs: mechanical advantage buys force by spending distance, a heavy arm steals energy from the payload, and drag is what creates a best projectile mass. Those conclusions are robust. The specific numbers are not predictions about any real machine.",
      "manual_x_dens": "Stone mass and stone size are separate controls, so the tool will let you build an object with a density no material has, and will then simulate it perfectly happily. It tells you when you have done that, but it does not stop you. Real materials come with a density attached, and it is worth remembering that choosing a size chooses a mass too.",
      "manual_x_drag": "Drag uses a single constant coefficient for a smooth sphere. Real projectiles tumble, and their drag changes as they do.",
      "manual_x_fric": "One efficiency figure stands in for all the friction in the winch. A real crew would feel it vary through the pull.",
      "manual_x_tors": "Torsion springs are modelled as linear. Real twisted sinew is strongly nonlinear, loses energy internally on every shot, and behaves differently in damp weather.",
      "manual_x_treb": "The trebuchet is modelled with one degree of freedom. A real one is a double pendulum, and the moment the sling releases is a genuinely hard problem this model does not solve.",
      "meet_note": "The engines are not new machines. They are these six, bolted together.",
      "meet_title": "Where you meet this machine",
